AMANAH AI - HOW TO RUN THE VERIFICATION PIPELINE ON GOOGLE COLAB
=================================================================

Notebook : amanah_ai_verification_pipeline.ipynb  (in this folder)
Purpose  : runs the complete Python pipeline (detection, retrieval, alignment, decision, source-backed correction,
           abstention) outside the web page, for inspection, batch runs and reproducing the reported numbers.
Runtime  : CPU only. No GPU is needed. Python 3.10 or newer (Colab's default runtime is fine).
Cost     : none. The pipeline uses only the Python standard library. The optional LLM step needs your own API key.


1. FILES YOU NEED
-----------------
From the GitHub repository, keep these two files at hand (about 10 MB in total):

    index/quran.idx.gz
    index/hadith.idx.gz

Alternative: data/quran.json and data/hadith.json (about 49 MB). The notebook then builds the two indexes itself
(roughly 20 seconds on Colab).


2. OPEN THE NOTEBOOK
--------------------
Option A - upload:
  a. Go to https://colab.research.google.com
  b. File > Upload notebook > choose amanah_ai_verification_pipeline.ipynb
Option B - from GitHub:
  a. File > Open notebook > GitHub tab > paste the URL of your public repository
  b. Open colab_verification_pipeline/amanah_ai_verification_pipeline.ipynb


3. CONFIGURATION
----------------
Cell "1. Configuration" has one switch:

    USE_LLM = False     # keep False unless you want the optional Ask & Verify step (section 8 below)

No other setting is required. The working folder is /content/amanah_ai (created automatically).


4. RUN
------
  a. Runtime > Run all.
  b. At cell "2. Provide the corpora", Colab shows a file chooser. Select the two index files
     (or the two JSON corpora). If you cancel, the notebook stops with a clear message.
  c. The module cells ("%%writefile ...") write the pipeline source files into the working folder.
     They print nothing important; each one is preceded by a short description.
  d. "4. Load the pipeline" prints the start-up time (a few seconds).
  e. Sections 5-11 then run the demo text, the bundled examples, the official-format export,
     the optional stress test and the anti-hallucination self-check.

Total time for Run all: about one minute on a standard Colab CPU runtime (measured locally on a standard CPU: 56 seconds,
including the stress test with 20 samples per kind).


5. WHAT YOU GET
---------------
  - A formatted Arabic report for the sample text (section 5) and a plain-text decision table (section 6).
  - One decision table per bundled example (section 7): VERIFIED, CORRECTED, UNSUPPORTED or HUMAN_REVIEW,
    the reason code, and the grounding flag.
  - Folder /content/amanah_ai/outputs/ with the three official files:
        task1A_predictions.tsv   spans (Question_ID, Span_Start, Span_End, Span_Type)
        task1B_predictions.tsv   Correct / Incorrect per quotation
        task1C_predictions.tsv   correction text, or the word "خطأ" when no source exists
    Download them from the Colab file browser (left panel > Files > outputs).
  - Stress-test rates per kind of quotation (section 10) and the final line
    "All N reports are grounded in the bundled corpora." (section 11).

To verify your own text, edit the variable `sample` in section 5 (or call verify("...") in a new cell).


6. OPTIONAL: ASK & VERIFY WITH AN LLM
-------------------------------------
  a. In Colab: click the key icon (Secrets) in the left panel > Add new secret.
     Name: OPENAI_API_KEY      Value: your own key      Enable "Notebook access".
     Never paste a key into a cell and never commit one to GitHub.
  b. Optional: set the environment variable OPENAI_MODEL in a cell before section 8
     (for example  os.environ["OPENAI_MODEL"] = "gpt-4o-mini").
  c. Set USE_LLM = True in cell 1 and re-run from cell 1.
The answer is cleaned of foreign-script characters and then verified exactly like any other text.
The notebook only calls the OpenAI-compatible endpoint with your key; nothing else leaves the notebook.


7. TROUBLESHOOTING
------------------
"Place index/*.idx.gz or data/*.json in the working directory"
    The upload was cancelled or the wrong files were chosen. Re-run cell 2 and select the files listed in step 1.

"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'verifier'" (or normalization, retrieval, ...)
    A "%%writefile" cell was skipped. Run all cells in order (Runtime > Run all).

"Index version mismatch; rebuild with index_builder.py"
    The index files come from a different version of the repository than the notebook. Upload the index files from the
    same repository version, or upload the JSON corpora so the notebook rebuilds them.

"ValueError: Input is too long"
    The pipeline limits one input to 20,000 characters. Split the text.

Section 8 raises "Missing secret" or a 401 error
    The secret name must be exactly OPENAI_API_KEY and "Notebook access" must be enabled. A 429 or 402 error means the
    provider's quota or balance is exhausted.

Colab shows "Session crashed" while loading the Hadith index
    Use Runtime > Restart session, then Run all. The standard runtime has enough memory (the Hadith index needs about 1 GB).

The Arabic report looks unstyled
    The report is HTML rendered by the notebook. Re-run section 5; if the output is still plain, open the notebook in a
    Chromium-based browser.

Results differ slightly from the web page
    They should not: the notebook embeds the exact same source files. Check that the notebook was regenerated after any change
    (python colab_verification_pipeline/build_notebook.py).


8. REPRODUCING THE REPORTED NUMBERS
-----------------------------------
The development-set evaluation (evaluate.py) needs data/islamiceval_dev_subset.jsonl and numpy. It is not part of the notebook; run it from
the repository:  python evaluate.py
The notebook's section 10 reproduces the stress-test rates (use a larger sample, for example 60 per kind, for the figures in the docs).


